@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@*
    #251 item 1 · THE ADRIFT LIFELINE, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of Map.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that was in
    the page, character for character. The one page member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME,
    which is what lets the markup move without a rewrite and lets the suite's source guards read it through
    MapMarkup exactly as before.

    The `@if` that opens it stays in Map.razor: the page keeps its paint order in one place, and a closed
    surface still renders nothing. IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs) — without it
    Blazor would put an automatic re-render back on every event in here, which the page turns off because
    the HUD's refresh belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules:
    `.map-adrift` and `.map-adrift-reopen` stay in Pages/Map.razor.css, where #1109 left them, and one of
    them — the adrift pulse — is animated by keyframes that have users on both sides of that partition, so
    moving it would be #1109's first named trap all over again.

    OpenRescueOffer is wired STRAIGHT to `@onclick`, so it crosses as an EventCallback and the PAGE is the
    receiver of the press rather than this surface (#1135 clause 2). That matters more here than almost
    anywhere: the press sets `_showRescueOffer`, which is the flag this surface's OWN `@if` reads to stop
    drawing the pill and the `@if` below it reads to put the rescue offer up — so a press the page never
    hears is a button that visibly does nothing.

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block used to sit inside `@if (…) { … }`,
    which is a Razor CODE block, and a code block is where its first statements are legal. The braces
    put the moved lines back in the context they were written in; they are outside the fences, so what
    the source guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the page.
*@
@* ── THE PAGE'S OWN NOTE ON THIS SURFACE, moved here with it (#251 item 1) and not touched by a
   word: it is the paragraph that stood above this block in Map.razor. It sits ABOVE the fence, not
   inside it, so that a source guard slicing the block by text goes on reading markup and not prose.
   ── *@
    @* #266: the adrift lifeline. The old strip rendered UNDER the masthead (z-order behind .map-topstack)
       and hid the one button a stranded captain needs — the #262 stranding proved it. Now the state
       SAYS itself on the shared #166 alert strip (above), the parrot proposes the tow, and the rescue is
       a real pop-up (below) with the terms visible. What remains inline is a clear, viewport-clamped
       re-open affordance (#253) that lives clear of the banner's no-button zone (#236) — shown only while
       adrift with the offer dismissed, so a captain who declined can always call the tug back. *@

@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ───────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <div class="map-adrift">
            <button type="button" class="map-adrift-reopen" title="Adrift — out of reaction mass. Open the rescue offer and read its terms." @onclick="OpenRescueOffer">
                🛟 Adrift — request rescue
            </button>
        </div>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}
